    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, so we're using these sine waves to find the frequencies. but how does the math actually \"know\" when it's hit a frequency that matches the signal? it feels like there's something almost magical about how the math just isolates one specific frequency and ignores all the noise from the others.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the \"magic\" lies in a concept called \"orthogonality.\" In mathematics, just as 90 degrees forms a right angle in geometry, orthogonality refers to the perpendicular relation between frequency components in Fourier analysis. Each sine wave in the Fourier series is orthogonal to all others. When the Fourier transform is applied, it \"leans\" only the part of the signal that corresponds to the sine wave's frequency while largely ignoring everything orthogonal to it. Consider it as having the ability to focus as if you're looking through a polarized lens: it lets through only the light polarized in the direction you're looking at and blocks others out. The process acknowledges and mathematically gives more \"hearing\" to the tones that match. It's not magic, but it is quite elegant and precise, which is why it seems magical.",
      "source": "target",
